We use the very cool complex numbers: let  $\cos \theta = \frac{z + 1/z}{2}$  and  $\sin \theta = \frac{z - 1/z}{2i}$ . Note that,
\begin{align*}
\cos k \theta &= \frac{z^k + 1/z^k}{2}
\sin k \theta &= \frac{z^k - 1/z^k}{2i}.
\end{align*}
Then we have that from infinite geometric series,
\begin{align*}
P &= \frac{2 \cos \theta}{5 + 4 \sin \theta}
Q &= \frac{4 + 2 \sin \theta}{5 + 4 \sin \theta}
\end{align*}
Then taking the ratio we have that,
\begin{align*}
\frac{2\sqrt{2}}{7} &= \frac{\cos \theta}{2 + \sin \theta}
\end{align*}
which yields  $\sin \theta = -\frac{17}{19}$  or  $\boxed{036}$ .

