This problem is kind of trolly, in the sense that only one  $(x, y, z)$  can possibly work. It's also the only way the problem could possibly work, because CRT never lets you get a handle on exactly how "big" the solution to a system of congruences is. 

Note that the first restriction is equivalent to  $1680 \mid N$ . It follows that none of 2, 5, 7 can divide  $N-3$ , which eliminates all  $x, y, z$  except for  $9, 11, 13$  — this must be the  $(x, y, z)$ !

Alright, now the grind. We essentially want  $k$  such that   $$ 560k \equiv 3 \pmod {1287}. $$   Obviously  $3 \mid k$ , so let  $k'=\frac k3$ , so now   $$ 560k' \equiv 1 \pmod {429}. $$   Reduce this via CRT:
\begin{align*}
560 k'&\equiv -k' \equiv 1 \pmod 3 
560 k' &\equiv -k' \equiv 1 \pmod {11} 
560 k'&\equiv k' \equiv 1 \pmod {13}.
\end{align*}
This means we essentially need to solve 
\begin{align*}
k'&\equiv -1 \pmod 3 
k'&\equiv -1 \pmod {11} 
k'&\equiv 1 \pmod {13},
\end{align*}
which is easy; in about twenty seconds we can find  $k' \equiv 131 \pmod {429}$  is the solution. Backtracking, this means that the desired   $$ N = 560 \cdot 3 \cdot 131 = 2^4 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7 \cdot 131, $$   so the answer is  $\boxed{148}$ .

We have:
\begin{align*}
&\log_2(\log_{2^a}(\log_{2^b}(2^{1000})))=0
\Rightarrow&\log_{2^a}(\log_{2^b}(2^{1000}))=1
\Rightarrow&\log_{2^b}(2^{1000})=2^a
\Rightarrow&2^{1000}=2^{2^ab}
\Rightarrow&2^ab=1000\end{align*}
Since  $v_2(1000)=3$ , we have  $2^a\mid1000$  iff  $a\in\{1,2,3\}$ , so the solutions are  $(a,b)=(1,500),(2,250),(3,125)$ . The answer is  $\boxed{881}$ .

Translate  $A$  to the origin and then use the complex plane. Then  $B = 1 + 2i\sqrt{3}$  and we wish to rotate  $AB$  about  $A$  by  $60$  degrees clockwise. To do this recall rotation by  $\theta$  counterclockwise about the origin is just multiplying by the point on the unit circle with angle  $\theta$ . Then we want to multiply by  $\frac{1}{2} - i\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$ . Multiplying  $B$  by this gives,
\begin{align*}
C &= (1 + 2i\sqrt{3}) \cdot \left(\frac{1}{2} - i\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right)
&= \frac{1}{2} - i\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + i\sqrt{3} + 3
&= \frac{7}{2} + i\frac{ \sqrt{3}}{2}
\end{align*} 
Shifting back  $C = \frac{9}{2} + i\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$ ,  $B = 1 + 2i\sqrt{3}$  and  $A = 1$ . Then the center has coordinates  $P = \frac{5}{2} + \frac{5\sqrt{3}}{6}$ . Thus  $xy = \frac{25\sqrt{3}}{12}$ .

