Let  $N$  and  $M$  be the feet from  $B$  and  $O$  to  $\overline{AL}$ , respectively. Let  $h=BN$ . Then  $OM=h\cdot\tfrac{4+\sqrt{13}}{4}$ . Since  $OL=\sqrt{13}$ , we have  $ML=\sqrt{(\sqrt{13})^2-(h\cdot\tfrac{4+\sqrt{13}}{4})^2}$ , so  $KL=2ML=\sqrt{52-h^2\cdot(\tfrac{4+\sqrt{13}}{2})^2}$ . Then the area of  $\triangle BKL$  is
\begin{align*}\frac12\cdot h\cdot\sqrt{52-h^2\cdot\left(\frac{4+\sqrt{13}}{2}\right)^2}&=\frac12\sqrt{52h^2-h^4\cdot\left(\frac{4+\sqrt{13}}{2}\right)^2} 
&= \frac12\sqrt{\left(\frac{26\cdot2}{4+\sqrt{13}}\right)^2-\left((4+\sqrt{13})h^2-\frac{26\cdot2}{4+\sqrt{13}}\right)^2} 
&\le \frac12\cdot\frac{26\cdot2}{4+\sqrt{13}}=\frac{26}{4+\sqrt{13}}=\frac{104-26\sqrt{13}}{3}\end{align*}
ergo  $\boxed{146}$ .

Okay so basically begin by noting at least one root is real. 

Case 1:  $3$  real roots. We can choose the roots from  $\{20, -20, 13, -13\}$  so we have  $\binom{6}{3} = 20$  ways here.

Case 2:  $1$  real root. Note the two complex roots must be conjugates so we let them be  $m + ni$  and  $m - ni$ . Note that we require,
\begin{align*}
(m + ni) + (m - ni) = 2m &\in \mathbb{Z}
(m + ni)(m - ni) = m^2 + n^2 &\in \mathbb{Z}
\end{align*}
Now break into subcases:

Subcase 1:  $m^2 + n^2 = 169$ . Then clearly we require  $m \in \pm \{0, 1/2, 2/2, \dots, 25/2\}$  which yields  $51$  solutions. 

Subcase 2:  $m^2 + n^2 = 400$ . Then we require  $m^2 \leq 400$  which yields  $m \in \{0, 1/2, 2/2, \dots, 39/2\}$  which yields  $79$  solutions.

Moreover after determining  $m$ ,  $n$  is uniquely determined in each subcase. Hence this case yields  $4 \cdot (51 + 79) = 520$  choices. 

Our final count is then  $520 + 20 = \boxed{540}$  cases.

