Thus, the inversion sends  $(ABC)$  to line  $BC$  and  $(XYZ)$  to the incircle (since  $X',Y',Z'$  all lie on the incircle). Thus, they are tangent, so we are done.**<span style="color:#f0f">Remark:</span>** This problem is another excellent example of *circular optimization*. First, you figure out to invert around  $(BIC)$ , and figuring out that  $A$  goes to the foot of the angle bisector which is not too difficult. then, you notice that the only way the problem statement could be true is if  $(XYZ)$  inverts to a circle that is tangent to  $BC$ . However, there is *only one circle* passing through both  $X$  and  $Y$  and is tangent to  $BC$ , namely the incircle. Then, we notice that  $Z$  is the furthest point away from  $L$  on  $(XYZ)$ , so it must invert to the closest point to  $L$ . Thus we can actually logically deduce where the image of  $Z$  must be in order for the statement to be true, and simply prove that it works, rather than having to "guess" it.

