Let  $S$  be the Sharky Devil of  $\triangle ABC$ , and  $D$  be the  $AB$  touchpoint, and  $M$  be the arc midpoint.
First we show that  $SXY$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$ .
Invert about  $(AIB)$ . We see that 
\begin{align*}
X&\mapsto X
Y&\mapsto Y
S&\mapsto D
(ABC)&\mapsto \overline{AB}
\end{align*}
As  $(DXY)$  is tangent to  $AB$ , se conclude that  $(SXY)$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$ .
Next, let  $Z'$  be the intersection of  $(SXY)$  and  $CI$  that is closer to  $C$ , and  $Z''$  be its image under the above inversion.
So 
\begin{align*}
MI^2 &= MZ'\cdot MZ''
\frac{MI}{MZ''}&=\frac{MZ'}{MI}
\frac{IZ''+Z"M}{MZ"}&=\frac{Z'I+IM}{MI}
\frac{IZ''+Z"M}{MZ"} -1 &=\frac{Z'I+IM}{MI} -1 
\frac{IZ''}{MZ"}&=\frac{Z'I}{MI}
IZ"\cdot MI &= Z'I \cdot MZ"  
IZ"\cdot MI &= Z'I \cdot (MI - IZ")
ID\cdot MI &= Z'I \cdot (MI - ID)
\frac{MI}{Z'I}&=\frac{MI-ID}{ID}
\frac{MI+Z'I}{Z'I}&=\frac{MI}{ID}
\frac{Z'M}{Z'I}&=\frac{\text{radius of  $(AIB)$ }}{\text{radius of  $(DXY)$ }}
\end{align*}
This ratio implies that  $Z$  is the center of homothety of the required circles so we are done.

The answer is  $kl$ .

Let the rectangle be  $ABCD$ , where  $AB, CD$  are vertically placed on the plane and  $AD,BC$  are horizontally placed.

In the following we refer to the vertices of the rectangles by mentioning vertices.

We called a segment basic if its endpoints are two vertices, it is parallel to the sides of the rectangle and that it contains no vertex in its interior. Notice that each vertex is the endpoint of three basic segments. We call a vertex left if it has no basic segements to its left, and define right, up, down vertices simiarly.

Consider a vertical segment  $l$ . It contains exactly one up and down vertex each. Moreover, notice that for a vertical line  $l_1$  just lie to the left of  $l$  and a vertical line  $l_2$  just lie to the right of  $l$ , the difference in the number of squares they intersect equals the difference of left and right vertices in  $l$ . Therefore, there are the same number of left and right vertices on  $l$ . For the  $i^{th}$  right vertex on the top we define its corresponding vertex to be the  $i^{th}$  left vertex on the top. For the up and down vertex on  $l$  we define its corresponding vertex to be itself.
