Now we define a horizontal path as follows: For a vertex on  $AB$ , we move along the horizontal segment connected to it until we reach another vertex. We move to its corresponding vertex and continue in the horizontal direction (If some vertices lie in the interior of vertical segments of a path we do not regard it as a vertex on the path, but we do for those lying in the interior of horizontal segments). It is easy to see that each vertex lies on exactly one horizontal path. Moreover, there are obviously  $k-1$  vertices on  $AB$ , hence exactly  $k-1$  horizontal path. Define vertical paths similarly. Then by symmetry there are  $l-1$  of them and each vertex lies on one of them. In other words, each vertex is the intersection of exactly one horizontal path and one vertical path. 

We now prove the key assertion.
<span style="color:#00f">**Claim.**</span>For each pair of horizontal path and vertical path, they intersect at exactly  $2$  vertices.
<u>Proof.</u>
Denote the horizontal and vertical path by  $A$  and  $B$ .

The endpoints of a vertical path lie on  $BC$  and  $AD$ , it partition the rectangle into two parts  $R_1$  and  $R_2$ , one conatining  $AB$  and the other does not. Therefore, any horizontal path must intersect it. Notice that the horizontal segments of the horizontal path cover every x-coordinate from  $AB$  to  $CD$ , moreover going along the path the  $x$ -coordinate is non-decreasing. Therefore,  $A$  must intersect  $B$  when  $A$  is entering of  $R_2$ , since  $B$  is the boundary of  $R_2$ . Moreover, when  $A$  leaves the boundary of  $R_2$  it will not intersect  $B$  again.

Therefore, it suffices to show that  $A$  intersect  of  $B$  at two vertices when it intersect it at the first and only time. Notice that the vertices of the horizontal segments of a vertical path must be either an up or a down vertex. Therefore, they can't be the first intersections of  $A$  or  $B$ .

Now we divide into two cases: 

<span style="color:#f00">Case I: </span>The first intersection of  $A$  and  $B$  is the interior point of a vertical segment of  $B$ . 
Then  $A$  will move to its corresponding vertex and leave  $B$ .  $\square$ <span style="color:#f00">Case II: </span>The first intersection of  $A$  and  $B$  is the endpoint  $X$  of a vertical segment of  $B$ .
Then  $A$  will travel in horizontal direction. Suppose the endpoint of the horizontal segment connected to  $X$  in path  $B$  is  $Y$  then every vertices in between  $X$  and  $Y$  including  $Y$  but not  $X$  is either an up or a down vertex. Hence  $A$  will move straight to  $Y$  and leave  $B$  there.  $\square$  $\blacksquare$ Now from the Claim we see that there are exactly  $2(k-1)(l-1)$  interior vertices. Each of these vertices is the vertex of  $2$  rectangles. Meanwhile there are  $2(k+l-2)$  vertices on the side of  $ABCD$  and  $4$  vertex on the corner, each corresponds to  $2$  and  $1$  rectangles. Let  $R$  be the number of rectangles then  $$ 4(k-1)(l-1)+4(k+l-2)+4=4R $$  Hence  $R=kl$  as desired.

