Let  $\omega$  be the circumcircle of  $ABCD$ . Let lines  $AB$  and  $CD$  intersect at  $X$ .

The condition  $\angle PDC + \angle PCB = \angle PAB + \angle PBC = 90^{\circ}$  means that the line  $\ell_P$  through  $P$  perpendicular to  $\overline{BC}$  is tangent to both  $\odot (APB)$  and  $\odot (CPD)$ . By radical axis on  $\odot (APB)$ ,  $\odot (CPD)$ , and  $\omega$ , the lines  $AB$ ,  $CD$ , and  $\ell_P$  are concurrent; i.e.  $X \in \ell_P$ , so  $\overline{XP} \perp \overline{BC}$ .

Morever,  $XP^2 = XA \cdot XB = \text{Pow}_{\omega}(X)$ . Similarly,  $\overline{XQ} \perp \overline{AD}$  and  $XQ^2 = \text{Pow}_{\omega}(X)$ .

Since  $XP = XQ$ , line  $PQ$  makes equal angles with lines  $XP$  and  $XQ$ . Since  $\overline{XP} \perp \overline{BC}$  and  $\overline{XQ} \perp \overline{AD}$ , line  $PQ$  makes equal angles with lines  $AD$  and  $BC$ .

