This problem is purely projective, so we can take a projective transformation so that  $ABCD$  is a square (this can be done because the problem gives us that  $ABCD$  is harmonic with the condition on  $P$ ). Note that we have defined  $E'$  as  $BR\cap\omega$ , and we'll show that  $AE'Q$  are collinear. Note that  $\angle EAC=\frac{1}{2}\widehat{EC}$  and  $\angle BRC = \frac{1}{2}(\widehat{BC}-\widehat{EC})=45^\circ-\angle EAC$ , so  $\angle EAC=45^\circ-\tan^{-1}(1/3)$ . We also have  $\angle QAC=\tan^{-1}(1/2)$ , so it suffices to show that  $\tan^{-1}(1/2)+\tan^{-1}(1/3)=45^\circ$ . We see that  $\tan(\tan^{-1}(1/2)+\tan^{-1}(1/3))=\frac{1/2+1/3}{1-(1/2)(1/3)}=1=\tan 45^\circ$ , as desired.

If  $E' = \overline{BR} \cap \omega \ne B$ , then  $-1 = (AC;BD) \overset{R}{=} (DC;E'A)$ , but also  $-1 = (DC;EA)$  so  $E' = E$ .

