Re-orient the problem so that it is  $A$ -symmetric rather than  $C$ -symmetric. Consider a  $-\sqrt{bc}$  inversion. Then the  $A$ -mixtillinear circle is sent to the  $A$ -excircle and  $P$  is sent to the touchpoint of the  $A-$ excircle with  $BC$ . Denote this point by  $D$ . Then it suffices to show that  $\measuredangle BAP = \measuredangle DAC$ . 

From here, we use complex numbers.  Let  $I$  be the incentre of triangle  $ABC$  and  $M_A$  the midpoint of major arc  $BAC$ . Set  $(ABC)$  as the unit circle and pick complex numbers  $x,y,z$  such that  $A = x^2, B = y^2, C = z^2, M_A = yz$ , and  $I = -(xy + yz + zx)$ . It is well known that  $P, I, M_A$  are collinear, so
\begin{align*}
P &= \frac{M_A - I}{M_A \overline{I} - 1} 
&= \frac{2yz + xy + xz}{-yz(\frac{1}{xy} +\frac1{yz} + \frac1{zx}) - 1} 
&= - \frac{ x(2yz+xy + xz)}{2x + y + z}
\end{align*}Let  $D'$  be the foot of the altitude from  $I$  to  $BC$ . It well known that  $D$  and  $D'$  are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of segment  $BC$ , so
\begin{align*}
D &= B + C - D'  
&= y^2 + z^2 - \frac{1}{2}\left(y^2 + z^2 - (xy + yz + zx) + y^2z^2 \left(\frac1{xy} + \frac1{yz} + \frac1{zx} \right) \right) 
&= \frac{y^2 + z^2 + xy + yz + zx - \frac{yz(x+y+z)}{x}}{2} 
&= \frac{x(y^2 + z^2) + x^2(y+z) - yz(y+z)}{2x} 
\end{align*}It suffices to show that  $\tfrac{B-A}{P-A} \div \tfrac{D - A}{C-A} \in \mathbb{R}$ . This is a straightforward computation. First, we conduct some simplifications:
\begin{align*}
\frac{B-A}{P-A} \div \frac{D-A}{C-A} &= \frac{ (y^2 - x^2)(z^2 - x^2)} {( - \frac{x(2yz + xy +xz)}{2x + y + z} - x^2)( \frac{x(y^2 + z^2) - x^2(y+z) + yz(y+z)}{2x}  - x^2)} 
&= \frac{ -2x(2x+y+z)(y^2 - x^2)(z^2 - x^2)}{ (x(2yz + xy + xz )+ x^2(2x + y + z))((x(y^2+z^2) + x^2(y+z) - yz(y+z) - 2x^3)} 
&= \frac{-(2x + y + z)(y^2 - x^2)(z^2 - x^2)}{(x+y)(x+z)(2x + y + z)(x(y+z) - x^2 - yz)} 
&= \frac{ -(y-x)(z-x)}{(x(y+z) - x^2 - yz)} .
\end{align*}Then we may check that
\[ \overline{\left( \frac{B-A}{P-A} \div \frac{D-A}{C-A} \right) } = \frac{ - (\frac1y - \frac1x)(\frac1z - \frac1x)}{\frac1x(\frac1y+\frac1z)-\frac1{x^2}-\frac1{yz}} = \frac{ -(x-y)(x-z)}{x(y+z) - yz - x^2} = \frac{B-A}{P-A} \div \frac{D-A}{C-A} . \]Done.

