WLOG  $a \ge b \ge c$ , let  $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = x, ab + bc + ca = y$ . Then  $2x = y(y+1)$ . Thus  $x-y = \frac{y(y-1)}{2}$ . As  $x \ge y$ , so  $\boxed{y \ge 1}$ . Now  $2(a-c)^2 \ge (a-b)^2 + ( b-c)^2 + (a-c)^2 = 2(x-y) = y(y-1)$ , implying  $\boxed{\frac{(a-c)^2}{x} \ge \frac{y-1}{y+1}}$ . Dividing both sides of the original inequality by  $\sqrt{x}$ , we obtain it suffices to show:
\begin{align*}
& \qquad ~~ 1 \le \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}} + \sqrt{\frac{y-1}{y+1}} 
&\iff 1 \le \frac{1}{x} + \frac{y-1}{y+1} + 2 \sqrt{\frac{y-1}{x(y+1)}} 
&\iff \frac{2}{y+1} \le \frac{1}{x} + 2 \sqrt{\frac{y-1}{x(y+1)}} 
&\iff 1 \le \frac{y+1}{2x} + \sqrt{\frac{(y-1)(y+1)}{x}} 
& \iff 1 \le \frac{1}{y} + \sqrt{ \frac{2(y-1)}{y} } 
& \iff \frac{y-1}{y} \le \sqrt{2} \cdot \sqrt{\frac{y-1}{y}}
\end{align*}
As  $\frac{y-1}{y} \le 1$  and  $\sqrt{2} \ge 1$ , so we are done!  $\blacksquare$

