We strong induct, as when  $n=1$  it is obviously true. Now consider the final two numbers, and call then  $x, y$ . Notice that we need to show  $(x+y)^4 \geq 2^{\frac{4n^2-4}{3}}$ . Notice that each of  $x, y$  are "composed" of  $a$  and  $b$   $1's$ , respectively, such that  $a+b=n$  (i.e. one of the initial values cannot contribute to both of  $x, y$ ). By our induction hypothesis we then have 
\begin{align*}
(x+y)^4 & \geq (2^{\frac{4a^2-4}{3}} 2^{\frac{4b^2-4}{3}})^4 
& \geq \left( 2 \cdot 2^{\frac{4 \left( \frac{a+b}{2} \right)^2 -4}{3}} \right)^4 
& \geq \left( 2 \cdot 2^{\frac{n^2-4}{3}} \right)^4 
& = 2^{\frac{4n^2-4}{3}} 
\end{align*}
finishing where we used Jensens and convexity.

