Found a solution with energy conservation:

Suppose the sphere has travelled a distance of  $x$  and moving at a velocity  $v$ . The potential energy lost from when  $t = 0$  is  $2Mgx \sin{\theta}$ . This has to match the kinetic energy gained. Clearly, the kinetic energy of the fluid is  $\frac{1}{2}Mv^2$ . The kinetic energy of the shell is  $\frac{5}{6}Mv^2$ . Adding the two together gives  $\frac{4}{3} Mv^2$ . Setting our two expressions equal, we see that
\[\frac{2}{3}v^2 = gx \sin{\theta}  \frac{dx}{dt} = \sqrt{\frac{3}{2} gx \sin{\theta}} 
dt = \sqrt{\frac{2}{3gx\sin{\theta}}} dx 
t = \int \sqrt{\frac{2}{3gx\sin{\theta}}} dx = \sqrt{\frac{8x}{3g\sin{\theta}}} \text{(notice that it matches the initial condition of (x, t) = (0, 0))} 
x = \frac{3}{8}gt^2 \sin{\theta}
a = \frac{d^2x}{dt^2} = \frac{3}{4}g\sin{\theta}\]

