We start with an important lemma.

<u>**Lemma 1**</u> The set of chords of the form  $\{a,b\}$  (where potentially  $a=b$ ) such that  $a+b=\chi$  for some fixed  $\chi$  is self-parallel.
<details><summary>Proof of Lemma 1</summary>We proceed by induction on  $n$ . The case  $n=3$  is trivial. Now suppose we are in a valid labelling with  $0,\ldots,n$ , and suppose the lemma is true for all  $m<n$ . If  $\chi<n$ , then ignore the  $n$  since it won't appear in any of the chords, and finish by the case  $n-1$ . Similarly, if  $\chi>n$ , then ignore the  $0$  since it won't appear in any of the chords. We finish by the case  $n-1$  again (as a valid labelling with  $1,\ldots,n$  is equivalent to one with  $0,\ldots,n-1$ ). Thus it suffices to consider the case  $\chi=n$ .

Again, by the lemma for  $n-1$ , the chords  $\{1,n-1\},\{2,n-2\},\ldots,\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil\}$  are parallel. Now these  $m=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$  chords divide the circle into  $m+1$  strips. If  $0$  and  $n$  are in different strips, then the chord  $\{0,n\}$  crosses one of these chords, a contradiction. Thus,  $0$  and  $n$  must be in the same strip. There are two cases:
<details><summary>Case 1 - The strip is one of the two edge strips</summary><details><summary>Figure 3</summary>[asy]
