Our goal is to show that Figure 6 is not possible. The key idea here is that replacing every number  $a$  with  $n-a$  and then reflecting the picture leaves it unchanged, except now  $0$  and  $n$  are on the other side of the strip. Thus, simply putting  $0$  and  $n$  on the other side of the strip also yields a valid configuration. Now suppose a chord below this strip was  $\{a,n-a\}$  and above was  $\{b,n-b\}$  with  $a\not=b\le n/2$ . Then, in at least one of the two configurations, we see that the chord  $\{0,a+b\}$  intersects the chord  $\{a,b\}$ , a contradiction. Therefore, we must have Figure 5, and all the chords are self-parallel, as desired.</details>
This completes the proof of the lemma.  $\blacksquare$ [/hide]

