<details><summary>Proof that Algorithm 2 works</summary>The proof of lemma 1 (or equivalently, just its statement) show that all labellings for  $n$  must be of the form described. All that's left to do is to show that all the labellings generated work. The proof of this is very slick.

By construction, all chords that add to  $n$  don't intersect. The chords that add to  $\chi<n$ , then the chords won't include  $n$ , so since its a valid labelling on  $n-1$ , it works. If they add to  $\chi>n$ , then the chords won't include  $0$ , so ignoring it and replacing every other  $a$  with  $n-a$ , and doing a reflection turns this into the same labelling on  $n-1$  that we started with, and the chords now have sum  $2n-\chi<n$ , so we finish by the previous argument for that case.  $\blacksquare$</details>

The key claim is the following.

<u>**Claim 3**</u> For every pair  $(a,b)$  with  $a+b>n$ ,  $a,b\le n$ , and  $\gcd(a,b)=1$ , there is a unique valid labelling on  $0,\ldots,n$  such that  $a,0,b$  are consecutive. Furthermore, every valid labelling is of this form.
<details><summary>Proof of Claim 3</summary>We proceed by induction on  $n$ , armed with algorithm 2 as our ability to relate  $n$  to  $m<n$  in a very strong way. The case  $n=3$  is trivial.
<details><summary>Proof that every valid labelling is of the described form</summary>Suppose  $a,0,b$  are consecutive. Firstly if  $a+b\le n$ , then the chords  $\{0,a+b\}$  and  $\{a,b\}$  intersect, which can't happen, so we must have  $a+b>n$ . Now, if  $a,b<n$ , then we finish immediately by the claim for  $n-1$ . Thus, WLOG  $a=n$ . Now, suppose the thing next to  $a$  away from  $0$  was  $x$ , so  $x,n,0,b$  consecutive. By lemma 1 for  $\chi=n$ , we see that  $b+x=n$  (note that every element is in a chord  $\{p,q\}$  with  $p+q=n$ ). Thus, we have  $n-b,n,0,b$  consecutive. By deleting  $n$  and using the claim for  $n-1$ , we learn that  $\gcd(b,n-b)=1$ , so  $\gcd(b,n)=\gcd(b,a)=1$ , as desired.</details>

Now suppose we have  $(a,b)$  satisfying the desired conditions. We'll show that a unique valid labelling exists with  $a,0,b$  consecutive. There are two cases.

<details><summary>Case 1: a,b<n</summary>WLOG  $a=\max\{a,b\}$ . Then by the claim for  $a$ , there exists a single configuration for  $0,\ldots,a$  with  $a,0,b$  consecutive. Since  $a+b>n$ , when applying the algorithm for the first time, we must have that  $0$  is in a middle strip. This forces the position of  $a+1$  to be unique, and on the other side of the strip, so in particular, not consecutive with  $0$ . As we keep continually applying the algorithm, we see that since  $a+b$  is just too big, we always default to the second case, with  $0$  being in a middle strip. This then forces a unique choice for the next value, and preserves  $a,0,b$  consecutive. This proves the claim in this case.</details>
<details><summary>Case 2: n∈{a,b}</summary>WLOG  $a=n$  (the other case follows in a similar fashion).Firstly, suppose a valid labelling exists. As shown before, this must mean that  $n-b,n,0,b$  are consecutive. Ignoring  $n$ , we have a valid labelling for  $n-1$ , and since  $\gcd(n-b,b)=\gcd(a,b)=1$ , there is only one labelling with  $n-b,0,b$  consecutive (by the claim for  $n-1$ ). Applying the algorithm, we see that we are in the first case ( $0$  on edge strip), so the only two possibilities for  $n$  are adding it so that either  $n-b,n,0,b$  or  $n-b,0,n,b$  are consecutive. The latter case does not have  $a,0,b$  consecutive, so the choice of  $n$  is unique, from a unique starting point, yielding a unique outcome.

Now, we construct a labelling (very similar to what we did just above, but we're repeating the ideas here for completeness). Start from a labelling for  $n-1$  with  $n-b,0,b$  consecutive (this exists by the claim for  $n-1$ ). Now, just add  $n$  in so that  $n-b,n,0,b$  consecutive and everything else is the same. By the algorithm, this works, and is a valid labelling. This completes the proof of the claim in this case.</details></details>

Of course, one could finish the problem here by simply counting the number of pairs  $(a,b)$  that satisfy the condition of claim 3, but here is a slightly more motivated way to finish.
<details><summary>The finish</summary>We prove the problem by induction on  $n$ , with the case  $n=3$  being trivial. When applying algorithm 2, we see that the only configurations for  $n-1$  that generate  $2$  labellings for  $n$  are the ones with  $a,0,b$  consecutive and  $a+b=n$ . All other labellings produce exactly one labelling for  $n$ . By the claim, the  $(a,b)$  that work are exactly the ones with  $\gcd(a,b)=1$ , so the number of new labellings we are adding is the number of solutions to  $a+b=n$  and  $\gcd(a,b)=1$ . This finishes by induction.</details>**Remarks** The finish suggests that there is probably no nice bijection from the solutions to labellings, as at each step some labellings expand to two symmetric labellings and the others just stay. We would have to break symmetry to assign different solutions to the two symmetric labeling.

Also, the only property of gcd that we used was that  $\gcd(n-b,b)=\gcd(b,n)$ . This kind of suggests that this could be some sort of model for executing the Euclidean algorithm, but I'm not really sure what I even mean.

