First, I prove a lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $a_i \le 2n$  for all  $1 \le i \le n.$  
*Proof.* Suppose for the sake of contradiction that for some  $1 \le i \le n,$   $a_i \ge 2n+1.$  Then, we know that
\begin{align*}
    2n+1 &\le a_i 
    &\le a_n 
    &\le a_1+n,
\end{align*}
so we get  $a_1 \ge n+1,$  from which it results that  $a_i \ge n+1$  for all  $1 \le i \le n.$  However, taking  $i=a_1,$  this means that   $$ a_{a_i} \ge n+1 $$   which contradicts the given condition that   $$ a_i \le n+i-1 \ \forall \ 1 \le i \le n. $$   Hence, the original supposition that  $a_i \ge 2n+1$  for some  $1 \le i \le n$  must have been false, concluding the proof of this lemma.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, let us create a bar graph with the values of  $a_1, \ldots, a_n$  and overlay a bar graph with the values of  $a_1, \ldots, a_n$  again but flipped across the line  $y=x$  and then shifted to the left by  $n$  units. For example, for  $n=5,$  a bar graph could look like:

