By the lemma, it is evident that the total sum of  $a_1, \ldots, a_n$  is equal to the total area of the two bar graphs that is in the bottom-right  $n \times n$  square of the grid that the bar graphs are overlaid on, where the grid is shaped to contain all of the bars with as small an area as possible. Therefore, it suffices to show that no two bars overlap. 

Now, by the condition that   $$ a_1 \le a_2 \le \cdots \le a_n, $$   it in fact suffices to show that for all  $1 \le i \le n,$  the horizontal bar for  $a_i$  does not overlap the vertical bar for  $i.$  This is because it is obvious that the  $j$  horizontal bar for all  $a_i+1 \le j \le n$  does not overlap the  $i$  vertical bar (since those bars are strictly above the  $i$  vertical bar), and the weakly increasing condition ensures that the  $j$  horizontal bar for all  $1 \le j \le a_i-1$  does not overlap the  $i$  vertical bar either. 

Suppose for the sake of contradiction that the  $a_i$  horizontal bar overlaps the  $i$  vertical bar for some  $1 \le i \le n.$  Then, we know that   $$ a_{a_i} \ge n+i. $$   However, this directly violates the condition that   $$ a_{a_i} \le n+i-1 \ \forall \ 1 \le i \le n, $$   contradiction. Hence, we are done.  $\square$

Notice that for any  $k$ , \[a_{a_k+1}+\dots+a_{a_{k+1}} \le (a_{k+1}-a_k)(n+k).\] Hence, we can write the equations:
\begin{align*}
a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{a_1} &= a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{a_1} 
a_{a_1+1}+\dots+a_{a_2} &\le (a_2-a_1)(n+1) 
a_{a_2+1}+\dots+a_{a_3} &\le (a_3-a_2)(n+2) 
&\vdots
\end{align*}
Summing these results in a telescopic expression, which reduces to  $a_1+\dots+a_n \le n^2$  as required.  $\square$

