\begin{align*}
	\angle TRC = \angle Y'CN + \angle CY'N = 2\angle CY'T = 2\angle CAT = \angle BAC,
\end{align*}
implying that  $\overline{AB} \parallel \overline{Y'T}$ . As  $Y$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{Y'T}$ , it follows that  $\overline{YM}$  is the common perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{Y'T}$ . This implies that our redefined  $Y$  is the same as the given one, proving the claim.  $\blacksquare$ Symmetrically, we find that  $AMXT$  is an isosceles trapezoid as well. Hence,  $MXYN$  is an isosceles trapezoid, implying that  $K = \overline{XY} \cap \overline{MN}$  lies on the shared perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{XY}, \overline{MN}, \overline{AT}$ . Thus,  $KA = KT$ , as desired.  $\Box$

