The angle condition in the problem implies that  $PB$  is tangent to  $\odot(ABC)$  and  $AB$  is tangent to  $\odot(DRB)$ . We can very easily prove that  $\triangle ABQ \sim \triangle ACB$  and  $\triangle PBA \sim \triangle PCB$ . Apply Power of a Point to also get that  $PD$  is tangent to  $\odot(ADC)$  which in turn gives  $\triangle PDA \sim \triangle PCD$ . Also  $DQCR$  is cyclic because 
    \[AD \cdot AR = AB^2 = AQ \cdot AC.\]
From the cyclicity,  $\triangle AQR \sim \triangle ADC$  follows which is probably the most critical observation. Now from  $\triangle ABQ \sim \triangle ACB$  we have that
    \begin{align}
        \overline{BQ} = \frac{AB \cdot BC}{BC}. \label{1}
    \end{align}
The rest of the length chase will be focused on finding  $\overline{QR}$  in terms of  $AB$ ,  $BC$  and  $CA$ . By using  $\triangle AQR \sim \triangle ADC$ , we have
    \[\overline{QR} = \frac{AQ\cdot DC}{AD}.\]
    From here on, it's mere similar triangles and some calculations.
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{QR} & = \frac{AQ\cdot AC \cdot DC}{AD \cdot AC} 
                      & = \frac{AB^2 \cdot CD}{AC \cdot AD} \qquad \text{( $AB$  tangent to  $\odot(DRB)$ )}  
                      & = \frac{AB^2}{AC} \times \frac{DC}{AD} 
                      & = \frac{AB^2}{AC} \times \frac{PD}{PA} \qquad \text{( $\triangle PDA \sim \triangle PCD$ )} 
                      & = \frac{AB^2}{AC} \times \frac{PB}{PA} 
                      & = \frac{AB^2}{AC} \times \frac{BQ}{QA} \qquad \text{(Angle Bisector Theorem in  $\triangle QBP$ )} 
                      & = \frac{AB^2}{AC} \times \frac{BC}{AB} \qquad \text{( $\triangle ABQ \sim \triangle ACB$ )} 
                      & = \frac{AB \cdot BC}{AC} = \overline{QB}
    \end{align*}
and thus the solution is complete in all its majesty.  $\blacksquare$

