\end{align*}
Therefore  $a \ge f(a)$ . So  $f(a)=a$ .
Our only solution is  $f(x)=x$  for all  $x$ .

We claim that the only solution is  $\boxed{f(n) = n}$ . It is easy to check that this satisfies the given equation.

Denote by  $P(m, n)$  the given assertion. 
	 $P(2, 2)$  gives 
\begin{align*}
4 + f(2) \mid 2f(2) + 2.
\end{align*}
Then, 
\begin{align*}
4 + f(2) & \mid (2f(2) + 2) - 2(4 + f(2)) 
4 + f(2) & \mid -6 
4 + f(2) & \mid 6.
\end{align*}
This implies that
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(2) & = & 2. 
\end{eqnarray*}
	 $P(2, 1)$  gives
\begin{align*}
4 + f(1) & \mid 2f(2) + 1 
4 + f(1) & \mid 5.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(1) & = & 1.
\end{eqnarray*}

Now,  $P(n, n)$ , for  $n > 1$  a positive integer gives
\begin{align*}
n^2 + f(n) & \mid nf(n) + n.
\end{align*}
This implies that 
\begin{eqnarray*}
n^2 + f(n) & \leq & nf(n) + n 
n(n - 1) & \leq & (n - 1)f(n) 
n & \leq & f(n).
\end{eqnarray*}

Finally,  $P(1, n)$  for  $n$  a positive integer gives
\begin{align*}
1 + f(n) & \mid 1 + n.
\end{align*}
Hence,
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 + f(n) & \leq & 1 + n 
f(n) & \leq & n \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}

Thus,  $f(n) \leq n$  and  $f(n) \geq n$ , so we must have  $f(n) = n$ , as claimed. This completes the proof.  $\Box$

