If  $n$  is even, then the above expression becomes   $$ 64\equiv225\pmod{2n+7}, $$   or  $2n+7\mid 225-64=161=7\cdot23,$  forcing us to have  $n=\frac{23-7}2=8$  as  $2n+7$  is prime and  $n>0.$  If  $n$  is odd, then we instead have   $$ -64\equiv225\pmod{2n+7}, $$   so that  $2n+7\mid 225+64=289=17^2,$  implying that  $n=\frac{17-7}2=5$ ; again,  $n$  is prime. It is easy to check that both  $n=5$  and  $n=8$  work; combined with the solution  $n=1$  for composite  $2n+7$ , we conclude that the only values of  $n$  for which  $2n+7\mid n!+1$  are  $1$ ,  $5$ , and  $8.$   $\blacksquare$

