For each pair  $(i, j)$  such that  $i \nmid j$ , we replace  $(x_i, x_j)$  with either  $(0, x_i+x_j)$  or  $(x_i+x_j, 0)$ , whichever increases the value of  $f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n) = \textstyle \sum_{(i, j) \in T}x_ix_j$ . We know that exactly one of the two increases this value because\begin{align*}f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_i, \cdots, x_j, \cdots, x_n) = \frac{1}{2}\left(f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, 0, \cdots, x_i+x_j, \cdots, x_n) + f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_i+x_j, \cdots, 0, \cdots, x_n)\right).\end{align*}Thus the maximum value of  $f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n)$  is reached when there do not exist two distinct nonzero  $x_i$  and  $x_j$  such that  $i \nmid j$ , since then we may perform the operation again. Hence
\begin{align*}
f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n) &\le f(x_1, 0, 0, \cdots, 0, x_{a_1}, 0, \cdots, 0, x_{a_2}, 0, \cdots, 0, x_{a_m}, 0, \cdots, 0)
 &= x_1x_{a_1}+x_1x_{a_2}+\cdots+x_1x_{a_m}+x_{a_1}x_{a_2}+x_{a_1}x_{a_3}+\cdots+x_{a_{m-1}}x_{a_m}
 &= \frac{1}{2}\left((x_1+x_{a_1}+x_{a_2}+\cdots+x_{a_m})^2 - x_1^2-x_{a_1}^2-x_{a_2}^2-\cdots-x_{a_m}^2\right)
 &\le \frac{1}{2}\left(1 - \frac{1}{m+1}\right)
\end{align*}
since  $(x_1^2+x_{a_1}^2+\cdots+x_{a_m}^2)(m+1) \ge (x_1+x_{a_1}+\cdots + x_{a_m})^2 = 1$  by Cauchy-Schwarz. Hence, if we want to maximize  $f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n)$ , we have to maximize  $m$ . Clearly  $m$  is maximized when  $a_1 = 2, a_2 = 4, a_3 = 8, \cdots, a_k = 2^k$ , so the maximum value of  $f(x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n)$  is\[\frac{1}{2}\left(1 - \frac{1}{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1}\right).\]
Equality holds when  $a_k = 0$  if  $k$  is not a power of  $2$  and  $a_k = \frac{1}{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1}$  otherwise.

