Let  $E_i$  be the expected amount of additional cents that Richard needs to get an integer number of dollars. We will be considering modulo  $100$ . For  $0<i<100$ , we have

\begin{align*}
E_i&=\frac{1}{4}(E_{i+1}+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{i+5}+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{i+10}+10) + \frac{1}{4} (E_{i+25}+25) 
&=\frac{E_{i+1}+E_{i+5}+E_{i+10}+E_{i+25}+41}{4}
\end{align*}

Summing across all  $99$  values of  $i$ , terms cancel out and we are left with 

\[E_1 + E_5 + E_{10} + E_{25} = 4059\]
\[\implies E_{0} = \frac{(E_1+1) + (E_5+5) + (E_{10}+10) + (E_{25}+25)}{4} = \boxed{1025}\]

Let  $E_n$  be the expected value of additional cents needed for an integer when the number of cents you have is  $n \mod{100}$  if it is not the starting position. Clearly,  $E_0=0$ . We will call the starting position  $E_S$ . It suffices to calculate  $E_S$ . 
\textbf{Claim: } $E_S=\frac{E_1+E_5+E_{10}+E_{25}+41}{4}$ \textbf{Proof: }
In the starting position, there is a  $\frac{1}{4}$  chance that you will have  $1$  cent,  $5$  cents,  $10$  cents, and  $25$  cents. So the expected value is:
\begin{center}
     $E_S=\frac{1}{4}(E_1+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_5+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{10}+10)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{25}+25)=$      $\frac{E_1+E_5+E_{10}+E_{25}+41}{4}$ \end{center}
So the claim is true and we must compute  $\frac{E_1+E_5+E_{10}+E_{25}+41}{4}$ Similarly, we can see that  $\forall i $  s.t  $1\leq i\leq 99$ ,  $E_i=\frac{E_{i+1\mod100}+E_{i+5\mod100}+E_{i+10\mod100}+E_{i+25\mod100}+41}{4}$ \begin{center}
     $E_S=\frac{1}{4}(E_1+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_5+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{10}+10)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{25}+25)$      $E_1=\frac{1}{4}(E_2+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_6+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{11}+10)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{26}+25)$      $E_2=\frac{1}{4}(E_3+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_7+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{12}+10)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{27}+25)$      $\vdots$      $E_{99}=\frac{1}{4}(E_0+1)+\frac{1}{4}(E_4+5)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{9}+10)+\frac{1}{4}(E_{24}+25)$ \end{center}
Summing up all the equations:
\begin{center}
     $E_S+E_1+E_2+\ldots E_{99}=E_1+E_2+\ldots +E_{99}+\frac{41}{4}\times 100$      $E_S=\frac{41}{4}\times 100=1025$ \end{center}

